[09:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Best practices for Cance 2.0 No prompting is basically a prompting almost like a director. Um, every prompt is basically a shot list collapsed into a paragraph. You should be specifying'

[09:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the subject, the action, the camera, the lighting, and the sound. And I have examples on the board we\'ll dive into in a little bit. Um, using like sort of vague descriptions can can get get averaged into usually a kind of generic'

[09:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'output. And with Cance 2.0, order really matters a lot in in uh creating great outputs. Um, the kind of order you want to write your prompt in is a subject, the subject itself of what\'s happening'

[10:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the scene, the action that\'s going to be occurring in that scene, the sort of camera movement um that you want to you want the video to have, um, the overall artistic style aesthetic of the video, and also any constraints um, for for'

[10:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that video. Um, the sequence of your words mirrors the priority of the output. So whatever you put first in the prompt um will be a lot more weighted higher when it\'s generated. Um and'

[10:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 're-ranging can change um what output is is um given to you. Um you want to make sure you\'re assembling correctly when you\'re doing like multiple images into a'

[10:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into you know the seance node. um you know mention saying something like character from image one, environment from image two, logo from image three in your prompt um can you know drastically improve the result that you get out of'

[11:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it instead of you know not tagging it, not saying anything about those images. Um you know a really strong thing about Cance 2.0 is image to video. A really strong starting image really preserves'

[11:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'identity in the composition and it helps you create a really incredible scene. Some other, you know, tips and tricks is being able to specify things of specifically stating the kind of camera'

[11:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you want like a three uh 35 millimeter anthropomorphic Kodak po portra warm low-key um you know giving getting specific in your prompt. It gives a lot of great constraints um to get a better'

[11:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'output than saying something vague like cinematic um which kind of gives the model permission to invent the kind of aesthetic that it delivers you. Um, another tip is to iterate like once'

[11:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at a time. Um, if you get a result that you don\'t like. Um, just change something about the prompt, the, you know, the camera, the lighting that you\'re describing, maybe even the speed of however, whatever you\'re describing,'

[12:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and then like regenerating it, you know, a couple of times before moving on to see if maybe I need to iterate some more, change my prompt a little bit more. Um, there\'s also t uh cdance 2.0 really respects timecoded shots. Um so'

[12:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if you say in you know 0.4 seconds describe the scene at 4.8 seconds describe the scene up to 15 um inside of a prompt. Um each of those segments'

[12:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gives its own sort of result the kind of mini video inside of that video. So that\'s how you can really create awesome multi-shot kind of clips. Um and also another tip the final tip is to start'

